.TH CHATTR 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
chattr \- change Windows file attributes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chattr
.RB [ \-R ]
.IR mode ...
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chattr
changes the Windows file attributes of each
.IR file .
Each
.I mode
is a
.B +
to add attributes, or a
.B \-
to remove them, followed by attribute letters:
.TP
.B r
Read-only.
.TP
.B h
Hidden.
.TP
.B s
System.
.TP
.B a
Archive.
.TP
.B t
Temporary.
.TP
.B n
Not content indexed.
.PP
Letters are case-sensitive.
Several modes may be given, as in
.BR "+hs \-r" ,
and together they make one change applied to every
.IR file .
Of these six attributes, those that no mode names are left unchanged.
For other attributes, see
.BR NOTES .
Adding and removing the same attribute is an error, reported as
.BR "can\(aqt set and unset a flag" ,
and then no file is changed.
.BR lsattr (1)
lists the attributes.
.PP
Arguments are read as modes up to the first that begins with neither
.B +
nor
.BR \- .
That argument and every later one is a
.IR file ,
even one that begins with
.B +
or
.BR \- .
.PP
Files are changed in order.
A
.I file
that cannot be found, or whose attributes cannot be set, is reported on
standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
chattr: can\(aqt stat \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
chattr: setting flags on \fIfile\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and the remaining files are still processed.
.SH OPTIONS
.B R
is recognized in any
.I mode
that begins with
.BR \- ,
so
.B \-Rh
recurses and removes the hidden attribute, and
.B \-R
may follow other modes, as in
.BR "+h \-R" .
.B +R
is an error.
Unless some mode begins with
.B +
or removes an attribute,
.B chattr
fails with
.BR "must use \- or +" ,
so
.B \-R
alone is an error.
There is no
.BR \-\- ;
write a first
.I file
that begins with
.B \-
or
.B +
as, for example,
.IR ./\-name .
.TP
.B \-R
Recurse: after changing a directory, change every entry in it, including
hidden entries and names that begin with a dot, and the entries of its
subdirectories.
Symbolic links and junctions are changed but not descended into.
A volume mount point is a directory, so the files of the volume mounted
there are changed too.
The entries of a directory that cannot be read are skipped without a
message.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
Files that could not be found or changed do not affect the status.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line: no
.IR file ,
no mode that begins with
.B +
or removes an attribute, an unknown attribute letter, or the same
attribute both added and removed.
A usage or error message is printed on standard error, and no file is
changed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a file read-only, and show the result:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ chattr +r config.h
$ lsattr config.h
\-\-\-\-\-r\-\-a\-\- config.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Clear the hidden and system attributes throughout a directory tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ chattr \-R \-hs backup
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a file hidden and system, and no longer read-only:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ chattr +hs \-r desktop.ini
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the e2fsprogs
.B chattr
on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
The attributes are Windows file attributes, not ext2 inode flags, and
the letters mean different things:
.B a
is archive, not append-only, and
.B s
is system, not secure deletion.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B =
mode: an argument that begins with
.B =
is a
.IR file .
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-V ,
or
.BR \-v ;
each is an unknown attribute letter.
.IP \(bu 2
Errors on individual files do not change the exit status.
.PP
.B chattr
passes
.B SetFileAttributes
only the six attributes above, so any other attribute that call can
set, such as offline, pinned, or unpinned, is cleared from every file
.B chattr
changes, even when no mode names it.
A mode of
.B +
alone adds nothing, so
.BI "chattr + " file
only clears those attributes.
Pinned and unpinned record whether cloud storage such as OneDrive keeps
a file on the device.
Other attributes that
.BR lsattr (1)
shows, such as compressed, encrypted, and sparse, cannot be changed.
.PP
The temporary attribute cannot be set on a directory.
The attempt fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" ,
and none of that directory's attributes change, though with
.B \-R
its entries still do.
.PP
A symbolic link or junction is changed itself, not its target.
Devices such as
.I /dev/null
are skipped without a message.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
A bare drive such as
.I C:
names the current directory of that drive.
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
A path of 260 or more characters, counting the current directory when the
path is relative, cannot be changed and is reported as
.B can\(aqt stat
like a missing file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR lsattr (1)
